{:edb/db-before-t 1649 :edb/db-after-t 1650 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534962 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:15:44.534Z" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124180 1100 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1)$." 13194139534962 false] [17592186124180 1100 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124180 1145 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 0 + 1)i + (0 + 4y + 0)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)i + 4yj \\\\\n\\nabla f(1, 1) &= (2(1) + 1)i + 4(1)j \\\\\n&= 3i + 4j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , 1 )}{\\Vert  \\mathrm{\\nabla } f ( 1 , 1 ) \\Vert} & = \\frac{3 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j}}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2} + {4}^{2}}} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{5} ( 3 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} )\\end{array}$$" 13194139534962 false] [17592186124180 1145 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124181 1100 "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$. Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4)$." 13194139534962 false] [17592186124181 1100 "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124181 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=x^2/9+y^2/16-1$. A normal vector is $\\nabla F=\\langle2x/9,y/8\\rangle$, which at $(0,4)$ equals $\\langle0,1/2\\rangle$. The normal line is therefore vertical: $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle0,4\\rangle+t\\langle0,1/2\\rangle$. Its Cartesian equation is $x=0$. Parametric form avoids division by the zero horizontal component." 13194139534962 false] [17592186124181 1145 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124182 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$." 13194139534962 false] [17592186124182 1100 "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124182 1145 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing\n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})k \\\\\n&= (4x - y)i - xj - 2zk \\\\\n\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt{2}) &= (4 \\cdot 2 - 3)i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k \\\\\n&= 5i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$" 13194139534962 false] [17592186124182 1145 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124183 1100 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$." 13194139534962 false] [17592186124183 1100 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124183 1145 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) \\\\\n0 &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)k \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2x - y}{x^{2} - xy})i + (\\frac{1}{y - x})j - k \\\\\n\\nabla f(2, 1, \\ln 2) &= (\\frac{2 \\cdot 2 - 1}{2^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1})i + (\\frac{1}{1 - 2})j - k \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}i - j - k \\\\\n&= \\langle \\frac{3}{2},-1,-1\\rangle \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{x - x_{0}}{v_{x}} = \\frac{y - y_{0}}{v_{y}} = \\frac{z - z_{0}}{v_{z}}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}\\frac{x - 2}{( \\frac{3}{2} )} = \\frac{y - 1}{- 1} = \\frac{z - \\ln   2}{- 1} \\\\ \\frac{x - 2}{3} = \\frac{y - 1}{- 2} = \\frac{z - \\ln   2}{- 2} .\\end{array}$$" 13194139534962 false] [17592186124183 1145 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322793 13194139534962 true] [17592186124379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322842 13194139534962 true] [17592186124379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322863 13194139534962 true] [17592186124379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322870 13194139534962 true] [17592186124379 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322877 13194139534962 true] [17592186124380 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322800 13194139534962 true] [17592186124380 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322849 13194139534962 true] [17592186124380 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322884 13194139534962 true] [17592186124380 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322891 13194139534962 true] [17592186124381 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322807 13194139534962 true] [17592186124381 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322856 13194139534962 true] [17592186124381 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322898 13194139534962 true] [17592186124381 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322905 13194139534962 true] [17592186124381 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322912 13194139534962 true] [17592186124382 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322814 13194139534962 true] [17592186124382 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322821 13194139534962 true] [17592186124382 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322828 13194139534962 true] [17592186124382 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322835 13194139534962 true] [17592186124382 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322919 13194139534962 true] [17592186124887 1100 "Consider the plane curve $6xy = 5x^{2} + y^{2}$. Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1, 1)$." 13194139534962 false] [17592186124887 1100 "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124887 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186124887 1145 "Write the curve as $F(x,y)=5x^2+y^2-6xy=0$. Then $\\nabla F=\\langle10x-6y,2y-6x\\rangle$, and $\\nabla F(1,1)=\\langle4,-4\\rangle$. The normal line passes through $(1,1)$ in this direction: $y-1=-(x-1)$, or $x+y-2=0$." 13194139534962 false] [17592186322793 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322794 13194139534962 true] [17592186322793 1089 "q-130271" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322793 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534962 true] [17592186322793 1100 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,4).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322793 1113 #uuid "2f1b31d1-29e5-5e40-8e1d-c102a9bf9bc8" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322793 1145 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322795 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322796 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322797 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322798 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322799 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1041 "selection" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322797 13194139534962 true] [17592186322794 1110 #uuid "93cba9eb-79c6-5b9c-a730-75a63e909d17" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322795 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534962 true] [17592186322795 1111 #uuid "f2f0fb4d-0acc-569d-889a-cccf1bebadd8" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322796 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534962 true] [17592186322796 1111 #uuid "8f366a3d-1d2f-506e-8ff1-34312d66b8ff" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322797 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534962 true] [17592186322797 1111 #uuid "e91dcffa-a379-583e-8af0-c9b6ddd4d440" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322798 1034 "-j" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534962 true] [17592186322798 1111 #uuid "5584c912-1c0c-54a6-9fdf-5f244c2f54e1" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322799 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534962 true] [17592186322799 1111 #uuid "7ef773d5-ffa7-519e-8b79-fb00aab7d750" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322800 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322801 13194139534962 true] [17592186322800 1089 "q-130724" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322800 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534962 true] [17592186322800 1100 "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322800 1113 #uuid "6d7c5aa5-5c3b-57a9-b613-006e91b71fa6" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322800 1145 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322802 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322803 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322804 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322805 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322806 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1041 "selection" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322803 13194139534962 true] [17592186322801 1110 #uuid "643db0dd-1b50-5f94-a712-f9414f9879ff" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322802 1034 "3x+2y=4" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534962 true] [17592186322802 1111 #uuid "edc8d069-2a81-5282-af66-4cea790ad699" 13194139534962 true] [17592186322803 1034 "x+